  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q27-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "When the digits of 14 are reversed to give 41, the result increases by 27.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p7-q27-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find all two-digit numbers that increase by 27 when their digits are reversed.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "14, 25, 36, 47, 58, 69.\n\nThe units digit must be 3 greater than the tens digit.",
          "worked": "Let $A$ be the tens digit and $B$ the units digit. The number is $10A+B$ and its reversal is $10B+A$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}10B+A&=10A+B+27\\\\9B-9A&=27\\\\B-A&=3\\\\B&=A+3\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $1\\le A\\le9$ and $0\\le B\\le9$, the possible tens digits are $1,2,3,4,5,6$. Hence the numbers are $14,25,36,47,58,69$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q27-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that there is no two-digit number that increases by 26 when its digits are reversed.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such number exists.\n\nReversing the digits changes the number by a multiple of 9, and 26 is not divisible by 9.",
          "worked": "Let $A$ be the tens digit and $B$ the units digit.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}10B+A&=10A+B+26\\\\9B-9A&=26\\\\9(B-A)&=26\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ are whole numbers, $9(B-A)$ is divisible by 9. But 26 is not divisible by 9, so no such two-digit number exists.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
